A||||5||Kelvin Vivian||Tire Treadwear #, FYI||||1||
Z||000000||Kelvin Vivian||11-25-2001||05:40 AM||||On the sidewall of most, if not all, tires there is a treadwear number... GoodYear Eagle GT IIs have a treadwear number of 420.  Another tire with treadwear 210 would wear twice as fast. The lower the treadwear number, the softer the tire.<P>I needed a new pair of tires for the rear of my 87 SE 4-cyl, the car had a set of 205-55-15s (86/7 GT rims) Eagle GT IIs on all four corners.  The rubber on the rear tires were "petrified" and worn past the little indicator in the grooves of the tires (the fronts are petrified too, but they've got a lot of life in them yet).  I've already gotten the back end of the 87 Fiero loose on a 270 degree freeway on-ramp during a light rain, I was able to catch it with a little counter-steer. With winter about here, I figured to be safe, I better pick up some rear tires.<P>At the store today, I picked up a set of rear tires with treadwear 260, not Eagle GT IIs. So now I have a car with two different sets of tires, it looks pretty ok. I would've gone with Eagle GT IIs, but, the store didn't have them readily available. I looked at the tires they had in my size and I figured, might as well as stick some grippy stuff in the rear. As of now, I think all four tires will eventually wear out at the same time, and when they do, the car will get the same new shoes on all four corners once again. Till then, grippy stuff in the rear during the winter and spring, then I'll move the petrified hard stuff to the rear during the summer and fall.<P>Keep an eye on your back end...<BR>KV<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=no_18&album_id=36084&image_id=0&courtesy=1"> <BR>The 87 SE 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||11-26-2001||12:50 PM||||Aaack!   My dunlops are rated at '200' treadwear (I think that's just 'gummy grippy') and I wouldn't put anything else on an exotic mid-engine car.<P>'warranty' tires are bad!  They're too hard!<P>And we wonder why so many drivers seem to be losing control of their cars all the time...<P>Be safe out there all!<P>PS: while I wouldn't recomend it, running with two brands of tyres front and rear probally won't hurt you too badly (if the grippier ones are on the rear)  However, if a tire is 'petrified' it needs to be replaced NOW!  Not next year, not tommorow!<BR>IT COULD EXPLODE AT ANY TIME!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||MR2KLR||11-26-2001||08:28 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> My Bridgestone Potenza RE-71's (195/55VR14) have a treadwear rateing of 160 and the Potenza RE-93's (215/45VR16) I just got at work for my GTZ wheels are reted 140  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Is that bad? Or cool? I guess they won't last for long  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.tripod.com/wawoonigootphet/images/af/3in1_vu1.jpg">  <A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Kelvin Vivian||11-26-2001||11:41 PM||||MR2KLR,<P>Those are some pretty soft tires you have, I'm pretty sure you can take corners at speed with a lot of confidence. A friend of mine who races at SCCA events uses tires with treadwear 30. I've seen some with a treadwear 0.<P>artherd, <P>I'll keep an eye on my front tires for any cracks in the sidewalls, as of now, just the tread blocks are "petrified".<P>kv<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=no_18&album_id=36084&image_id=3&courtesy=1">    <BR>88 GT, 5-spd<BR>87 SE 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||14
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||11-26-2001||11:44 PM||||My sixteen inch pep boys tires are 560! Car still handels well though. Arn't higher tread wear numbers for winter? Or is it the other way?||||reg||10
Z||000005||Nashco||11-27-2001||02:37 AM||||It's the other way, in most cases.  Winter tires tend to be a little softer (real tires, not the 24.95 specials) because they're exposed to a lot colder temps.  If you expect them to grip at all in the ice, they have to be pretty soft.<P>The higher the tread rating the harder the tire, almost every time.  Some better tread designs last longer with softer rubber, but you'll pay big bucks.<P>The tires that have a warranty are usually really hard so they'll hit that mileage mark, and as a result have crappy performance because they're not sticky and you'll screech around corners and on hard accels/decels.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
